 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "`active_app`, `g` and `request` are Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects that all read from one `contextvars.ContextVar`.\n\n- **`src/tessel/globals.py:40`**: `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext] = ContextVar(\"tessel.app_ctx\")` is the single source of the current context.\n- **`src/tessel/globals.py:41-62`**: each global is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"<attr>\")`. On every access the proxy calls `_app_var.get()` and returns one attribute of the resulting `AppContext`:\n  - `active_app` returns `.app` (the `Tessel` app object)\n  - `g` returns `.g` (an `_AppCtxGlobals` instance, `src/tessel/ctx.py:30`)\n  - `request` and `session` return `.request` and `.session`\n  - `app_ctx` returns the `AppContext` itself\n- **`src/tessel/ctx.py:260`, `class AppContext`**: `push()` binds the context with `self._cv_token = _app_var.set(self)` (line 433). `pop()` restores the previous value with `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (line 498).\n\n**Why it resolves correctly:** a `ContextVar` holds a separate value per thread and per asyncio task, so each concurrent request sees the `AppContext` it pushed. Because push and pop use a set/reset token, nested contexts unwind back to the outer one. If nothing has been pushed, the proxy raises the `unbound_message` error (\"Working outside of application/request context\").\n\nThe request context has been merged into `AppContext`. That's why `request` reads from the same var, and the old `request_ctx` name is now a deprecated alias for `app_ctx` (`globals.py:65-77`).",
